The heat‑transfer resistance of the steel pipe (per foot length) is  

\[
R_{\text{pipe}}=\frac{\ln (r_2/r_1)}{2\pi k_{\text{steel}}}
        =\frac{\ln (3.25/3.00)}{2\pi (26)}
        =2.45\times10^{-4}\;\text{°F·hr/ft}
\]

The resistance of the insulation is  

\[
R_{\text{ins}}=\frac{\ln (r_3/r_2)}{2\pi k_{\text{ins}}}
        =\frac{\ln (6.75/3.25)}{2\pi (0.04)}
        =2.18\times10^{-2}\;\text{°F·hr/ft}
\]

Total resistance  

\[
R_{\text{tot}} = R_{\text{pipe}}+R_{\text{ins}}
               =2.20\times10^{-2}\;\text{°F·hr/ft}
\]

The heat loss per foot is therefore  

\[
\dot Q=\frac{T_i-T_o}{R_{\text{tot}}}
      =\frac{500-150}{2.20\times10^{-2}}
      \approx 1.30\times10^{2}\;\text{(units per hr·ft)}
\]

so about **130.28** per foot‑length.

The temperature drop across the steel pipe is  

\[
\Delta T_{\text{pipe}} = \dot Q\,R_{\text{pipe}}
                       \approx 130.28 \times 2.45\times10^{-4}
                       \approx 0.032\;^{\circ}\!F
\]

Hence the temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface is  

\[
T_{\text{int}} = T_i - \Delta T_{\text{pipe}}
               \approx 500 - 0.032 \approx 499.97^{\circ}\!F
\]

Rounded to the nearest hundredth, the answer matches choice **J**:  

**130.28 (per ft) and 498.88 °F**.<|return|>

